## Data

| Document | Source | Pages | Chunks | Description |
|----------|--------|-------|--------|-------------|
| **DRR 2027** | SNCF Réseau | ~300 | 709 | Document de Référence du Réseau — network access rules, capacity allocation, pricing |
| **DRG 2027** | SNCF Gares & Connexions | ~150 | 146 | Document de Référence Gare — station access, service tiers, fee structures |
| **EPSF 2018** | EPSF / BEA-TT | ~100 | 157 | Railway safety investigation reports and regulatory guidance |

**Total corpus:** 1,012 chunks, ~500 pages of French regulatory text.

### Why This Corpus?

French railway regulation is dense, continuously evolving, and highly structured — an ideal stress test for RAG systems. Tables with tariff scales, multi-section legal references, and domain-specific vocabulary push chunking and retrieval strategies beyond what generic English benchmarks measure.

---

## Week 1: Data Ingestion & Retrieval Engineering

### PDF Parsing (Docling)

Raw PDFs are processed through [Docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling) to produce structured markdown with embedded page markers (`<!-- page: N -->`). A preflight step handles 2-up landscape PDFs (common in regulatory documents) by splitting them into single-page format using PyMuPDF.

### Chunking Strategy: Structure-Aware

Three document-specific chunkers implement **section-header-aware splitting**:

- **DRR chunker** (`chunking.py`) — detects chapter/section headings via regex, preserves section hierarchy in `section_path` metadata
- **DRG chunker** (`drg_chunking.py`) — adds table-aware splitting to keep tariff grids intact
- **EPSF chunker** (`epsf_chunking.py`) — handles safety report structure

Each chunk carries rich metadata:
```
chunk_id, document_id, text, token_count, section_path[], page_start, page_end
```

Max 512 tokens per chunk, tokenizer-aligned with the embedding model to prevent mid-word splits.

### Two-Stage Retrieval

```
Query → Embed (SentenceTransformer) → Qdrant Dense Search (Top-30)
      → Cross-Encoder Rerank (Top-10) → Select Top-5 for Generation
```

The system supports both **cross-encoder** (BGE-Reranker-v2-m3) and **causal LLM** (Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B) reranking, switchable via configuration.

---

## Week 2: Evaluation, Observability & API

### Retrieval Ablation Study (12 Configurations)

All 12 configurations were evaluated on the same 187-question gold benchmark. Stage 1 retrieves K=30 candidates (or K=10 when no reranker is present).

| Rank | Embedding Model | Reranker | Dims | HR@1 | HR@10 | Gold Recall | Misses | Latency |
|:----:|-----------------|----------|:----:|:----:|:-----:|:-----------:|:------:|:-------:|
| **1** | **EmbeddingGemma-300m** | **BGE-Reranker-v2-m3** | **768** | **82.4%** | **96.3%** | **93.3%** | **7** | **461 ms** |
| 2 | Multilingual-E5-Large | BGE-Reranker-v2-m3 | 1024 | 82.4% | 96.3% | 93.3% | 7 | 508 ms |
| 3 | EmbeddingGemma-300m | Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B | 768 | 74.3% | 96.3% | 92.8% | 7 | 1,439 ms |
| 4 | Multilingual-E5-Large | Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B | 1024 | 73.3% | 96.3% | 92.8% | 7 | 1,595 ms |
| 5 | BGE-M3 | BGE-Reranker-v2-m3 | 1024 | 79.7% | 93.6% | 90.7% | 12 | 403 ms |
| 6 | EmbeddingGemma-300m | *(none)* | 768 | 74.9% | 92.5% | 89.0% | 14 | **5 ms** |
| 7 | BGE-M3 | Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B | 1024 | 72.2% | 93.1% | 89.8% | 13 | 1,351 ms |
| 8 | Multilingual-E5-Large | *(none)* | 1024 | 66.8% | 92.0% | 87.8% | 15 | **4 ms** |
| 9 | BGE-M3 | *(none)* | 1024 | 66.8% | 90.9% | 87.2% | 17 | **4 ms** |
| 10 | Nomic-Embed-v1.5 | BGE-Reranker-v2-m3 | 768 | 76.5% | 86.6% | 82.4% | 25 | 471 ms |
| 11 | Nomic-Embed-v1.5 | Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B | 768 | 65.8% | 86.1% | 81.6% | 26 | 1,505 ms |
| 12 | Nomic-Embed-v1.5 | *(none)* | 768 | 54.0% | 79.1% | 75.0% | 39 | **4 ms** |

**Key findings:**

- **EmbeddingGemma-300m wins on efficiency:** matches E5-Large on accuracy with 25% smaller vectors and ~40% faster embedding
- **Reranker is non-negotiable:** BGE-Reranker boosts HR@1 by +7.5pp (Gemma), +15.5pp (E5), +22.5pp (Nomic)
- **Qwen3-Reranker is inefficient:** 3x slower than BGE with lower accuracy across all embedders
- **Nomic is unsuitable for French legal text:** 39 misses without reranker, worst-in-class HR@10

### End-to-End RAGAS Evaluation

Two generation models were compared using DeepSeek-Chat as an independent LLM judge on the same 187 questions and retrieved context:

| Metric | Gemma4-E2B (Ollama) | Qwen2.5-7B (4-bit) | Winner |
|--------|:-------------------:|:------------------:|:------:|
| **Faithfulness** | **0.967** | 0.916 | Gemma (+0.051) |
| **Answer Relevancy** | 0.770 | **0.890** | Qwen (+0.120) |
| **Context Precision** | **0.848** | 0.836 | Gemma (+0.012) |
| **Context Recall** | **0.913** | 0.911 | Tie (+0.002) |

**Why Gemma wins on faithfulness but loses on relevancy:** Gemma4-E2B strictly follows the system prompt's refusal constraint (*"Si la réponse n'est pas dans le contexte, indique que tu ne sais pas"*), generating conservative refusals when context is sparse. DeepSeek's relevancy metric penalizes these refusals even though they are factually correct behavior — a known limitation of LLM-as-a-judge evaluation.

### Failure Mode Analysis

All **7 remaining misses** (3.74% of 187 questions) were root-caused to **table fragmentation**: ASCII/Markdown tables split across chunk boundaries, separating headers from row values. This validates the evaluation methodology against real-world dirty data rather than synthetic benchmarks.

### API: OpenAI-Compatible FastAPI

The system exposes an OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions` endpoint with:

- SSE streaming support
- Page-level citations (`[page 30]`, `[pages 12-14]`)
- Multilingual system prompt (French context, user-language response)
- VRAM-aware model management (unloads retrieval models before loading local HF LLM on 8GB GPUs)
- Langfuse tracing for all retrieval and generation calls

### Observability

- **Langfuse** lazy client with auth check — traces all query embeddings, dense retrieval, reranker scores, and generation completions
- All intermediate artifacts persisted: per-item JSON/CSV metric tables, DeepSeek judge reasoning samples, timestamped audit logs
- **Judge cost tracking:** 187 questions produced **~748 DeepSeek judge calls** and **3.09M tokens** (~16.5k tokens/question). The **$1.11** figure is a cache-miss *ceiling* at historical `deepseek-chat` rates, not the billed invoice — shared RAGAS prefixes usually cache, so dashboard spend is typically much lower.

---

## Tech Stack

| Component | Selection | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|-----------|
| **Vector DB** | Qdrant (embedded, local) | Lightweight, fast, GPU-accelerated cosine search |
| **Dense Embedder** | `google/embeddinggemma-300m` | Best accuracy/efficiency tradeoff (768-dim, 40% faster) |
| **Reranker** | `BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3` | +7-22pp HR@1 improvement at ~450ms/query |
| **Generation LLM** | `gemma4-e2b` via Ollama | 0.967 faithfulness; peak VRAM **N/A** (Ollama process not measured by PyTorch) |
| **Eval Judge** | DeepSeek-Chat via RAGAS | Token-logged; billed cost usually below the cache-miss ceiling |
| **Observability** | Langfuse | Full LLM tracing, open-source |
| **API** | FastAPI + Uvicorn | OpenAI-compatible contract, SSE streaming |
| **PDF Parsing** | Docling + PyMuPDF | Structure-aware extraction with page markers |
| **Packaging** | uv + pyproject.toml | Deterministic builds, CUDA 12.4 wheels |

---

## Quick Start

### Prerequisites

- Python 3.10+
- [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) package manager
- [Ollama](https://ollama.com/) (for local LLM inference)
- NVIDIA GPU with 8+ GB VRAM (optional but recommended)

### Installation

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Sam1brahim/RAG-SN-IN.git
cd RAG-SN-IN

# Create virtual environment and install dependencies
python -m venv .venv
uv sync
```

### Run the Interactive CLI

```bash
# Windows
.venv\Scripts\python start.py

# Linux / macOS
.venv/bin/python start.py
```

If you run `python start.py` without activating the venv, the script will automatically detect this and relaunch itself using the project's virtual environment.

The CLI walks you through:

1. **Choose your pipeline** — Retrieval evaluation, full RAGAS evaluation, or launch the API server
2. **Select models** — Pick embedding model, reranker, and generation LLM from the pre-configured options
3. **Automatic indexing** — If the vector database is empty, chunks are automatically embedded and indexed with the correct vector dimensions for your chosen model
4. **View results** — All metrics, per-item scores, and summary statistics saved to `data/`

### Environment Setup (for RAGAS evaluation only)

Create a `.env` file in the project root:

```env
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=your_deepseek_api_key_here
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=your_langfuse_public_key    # optional
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=your_langfuse_secret_key    # optional
LANGFUSE_HOST=http://localhost:3000              # optional
```

### Run the API Server

```bash
uvicorn rag_sn_in.api.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

Connect [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui) to `http://localhost:8000/v1` for a production-grade chat interface with native citation rendering.

